Carafe: JumpTables reads a switch's tables from any committed readable memory, not only from the executable range of
the jump. Libraries built with GCC or MinGW (DXVK's d3d9.dll, Wine's d3dx9) keep their jump tables in .rdata, far
from the code, and those switches took 3.2 % of the samples of Carbon's race on FEX's lookup. The frontend asks the
new QueryGuestReadableRange, which WoW64 answers with NtQueryVirtualMemory, and keeps the last range it got. Up to
four instructions may now stand between the bounds check and the jump, as in GCC's "cmp; ja; lea; jmp [table]". The
index register is not traced through them: a wrong bound only reads a table too short or too long, and every jump
still compares the loaded target with the case's guest RIP before it takes the case.
MIT, as the files it changes.

